1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Tunku Sharif was born 1890 in Jelebu. At a young age he left Jelebu to stay with his uncle Tunku Mambang and Tunku Asiah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little was known about his young life as he was educated at the Malay College Kuala Kangsar
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1940s,Tunku Mohd Sharif  together with Dato Andika Ujang followed the late Yam Tuan Abdul Rahman to the Bukingham Palace in the United Kingdom when the latter was bestowed the KCMG which carried the title SIR by the then Br
2 CONC itish King George.The journey took weeks by sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tunku Mohd Sharif was the 1st Tunku Alang of Sri Menanti and also held the 1st comptroller's post of the royal household of NS.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandson to the late Yam Tuan Ulin, Tunku Mohd Sharif acted as advisor on the  "adats" and matters related to the royal household of NS during the reigns of Yam Tuan Muhammed and Yam Tuan Abdul Rahman.
2 CONT
2 CONT He played a prominent role in coordinating with the home guards that helped combat the communist insurgencies during the Emergency.He also sat with the Emergency committee comprised of British residents and the police that decided
2 CONC  on imposement and upliftment of curfews within the Kuala Pilah district.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tunku Mohd Sharif was known for his bravery and some claimed that he had the ability to stay under water for as long as 10 minutes.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also noted for his grace and agility when it came to dancing the joget . His favourite number was the "Serampang Laut"
2 CONT
2 CONT Clad in his favourite Safari khaki outfit, a colt .45 holster stuck to his waist and a walking stick he was feared most by the Palace staff for his uncompromised dicipline. He would tick off anyone whose leather shoes did not shin
2 CONC e . His famous punchline: " if you look neat and smart you'd be adored even if your pockets are empty BUT, if you look messy no one would get near you "
2 CONT
2 CONT Even his shirt buttons bore the KNS ( kerajaan NS ) logo as well as his OKA1 shoes,ties, mufflers and cuff links were imports of the UK.
2 CONT
2 CONT On hind side he was a bad loser .When the then Malayan football team  lost to the Burmese national side that was aired live , the radio became the 1st casualty as he would punch and toss it until it was broken !
2 CONT
2 CONT Beat him over a game of chess or checkers one would find the board and seeds flying all over the room.
2 CONT
2 CONT His spoken English was excellent.He'd take one to task if one spoke broken English or bad pronounciation to him eg. children sb pronounced as "chill-dren " and "pronounciation" sb pronounced as "pro nun si a shen ".Some would get 
2 CONC punished for uttering "farder" and  "marder" for father and mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT He even taught his children and close ones how to tie the "samping " and wear the "kopiah "
2 CONT
2 CONT On the health side he suffered from diabetics for more than 2 decades and finally in 1965 he succumbed to the illness.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was burried at the royal moseleum in Sri Menanti with full ' adat '
